---
Part III, Chapter 6: Fenced code
Language: Prose
---

# Fenced code

A plain block of code ends at the next heading. A heading is a run of one to six hashes followed by a space, so any line of code that starts that way ends the block, whatever its author meant by it. Most code never writes such a line. A shebang like `#!/bin/sh`, a C `#include`, and a Rust attribute like `#[derive(Debug)]` carry no space after the hash, so they stay code.

The lines that do end a block by mistake form a short list, but a real one:

- a comment in a shell script, a Python module, or a YAML file;
- a directive written as a comment, such as `# shellcheck disable=SC2086`, a Dockerfile's `# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1`, or Python's `# type: ignore`;
- a heading in a Markdown file that a section writes.

A literate loom rarely needs the comments, since its prose explains the code. The other two are not optional. A directive changes what a tool does, and a Markdown heading is the content of the file.

Code that must hold such lines is fenced, the way Markdown fences any code. The fence is a mark every reader of Markdown already knows, so Loom asks no one to learn a mark of its own for a rare case.

## An arrow, then a fence

Fenced code opens like every block of code, with an arrow on its own line. On the first line after the arrow that is not blank comes a fence: three backticks, optionally followed by the section's language. Every line after the fence is code, until a fence that matches it closes the block.

Inside fenced code, a line that starts with `#`, `=>`, or `~` is code like any other, and so is a line of dashes. Anchors and warps still compose, so fenced code draws in other sections just as a plain block does. A quote composes nothing, so composition is what tells fenced code from a quote.

The closing fence ends the block, and prose follows on the next line, with no tilde to close the code.

Part XI's install script shows the case this serves. The script opens with a comment that says what it does and how to run it, and the comment belongs in the file, since a person who opens `install.sh` reads it there. Its shebang stays code, because no space follows the hash, but nearly every line of the comment starts with a hash and a space. A plain block would end at the first of those lines and read the rest as headings. Fenced, the block holds them all:

````loom
=>
```sh
#!/bin/sh
# Installs Loom's standalone executable and puts it on PATH.
#
#   curl -fsSL https://github.com/athrio-com/loom/releases/latest/download/install.sh | sh

set -eu
```
````

## A fence alone is a quote

A fence in prose that does not follow an arrow is a quote. Its lines are shown and never composed, and none of them is a mark. Part XI, Releasing Loom, quotes this way the one line a person types to install Loom. Part VII, Languages as packages, quotes a diagram of how editor requests reach each language. Neither belongs in any file the book writes, and the fence keeps them out.

A fence inside a plain block of code belongs to the code. A section that writes a Markdown file may hold fences of its own, and they are part of the file it writes.

So a reader tells the two kinds of fence apart by the line above them. An arrow above a fence opens fenced code. Prose above a fence opens a quote.

## Long fences hold short ones

A closing fence starts at the column its opening fence started at, and it is at least as long. Nothing follows its backticks but spaces. A block of code that holds three backticks of its own, such as a Markdown file or a test that builds a loom, therefore opens with a fence of four, and only another four closes it. The rule holds for quotes as well, so a loom can quote a loom that holds fenced code, as this chapter does above.

The column matters as much as the length. A TypeScript template string may hold an indented line of backticks, and that line must not close the block around it. Every reader of a loom keeps the rule the same way: the classifier and the tokeniser through `openingFence` and `closesFence`, the weave, the editor's line scanner, and the website that draws a loom.

## The fence's label

A fence may carry a label, the language name Markdown writes after the backticks, and it may leave it off. The label is optional because Loom already knows each section's language: from the heading's specifier first, then the extension of the file its sink names, then the chapter's `Language:`. A fence without a label is read exactly as a fence with one.

A label tells Loom nothing new, so it must never contradict what Loom knows, and above all never the specifier. A label agrees when it names the section's language, or a file extension that maps to it, so `ts` and `typescript` both label a TypeScript section. A label that names another language is a fault, so one block never has two languages. A `{Bash}` section in a chapter of TypeScript takes `bash` on its fence, never `ts`.

The label serves the readers outside Loom. GitHub, and any other Markdown viewer, colours a fenced block by its label.

## What can go wrong

Three faults keep fenced code honest:

- **A fence that never closes.** Fenced code with no closing fence would swallow the rest of the loom, so its opening fence carries an `UnclosedFence` error, and the editor underlines it where the code began.
- **A label against the language.** A fence whose label names a language other than the section's, the specifier's when the heading has one, carries a `FenceLabelAgainstLanguage` error, on the label. A fence with no label has nothing to check.
- **A heading against code.** A heading written directly under a line of code most likely ended the code by mistake, where a `#` comment was meant. The `HeadingAgainstCode` warning marks it, and its message names the fix: fence the block.
